### 7.1 Agent definition

Each agent should have the following attributes:

| Field          | Purpose                   |
| -------------- | ------------------------- |
| agent_id       | Unique identifier         |
| name           | Agent name                |
| role           | Functional responsibility |
| description    | Agent purpose             |
| model_provider | Model provider            |
| model_name     | Selected model            |
| system_prompt  | Agent instructions        |
| capabilities   | Permitted capabilities    |
| tools          | Authorized tool access    |
| permissions    | Execution permissions     |
| status         | Current lifecycle status  |
| version        | Configuration version     |
| created_at     | Creation time             |
| updated_at     | Last update               |

### 7.2 Agent lifecycle

Lifecycle states

- Created.
- Configured.
- Validating.
- Ready.
- Active.
- Paused.
- Failed.
- Disabled.
- Archived.

Each transition must be validated.

### 7.3 Agent execution model

An agent execution should follow this pattern:

```
Receive Task
     |
     v
Validate Task
     |
     v
Check Agent Permissions
     |
     v
Retrieve Authorized Context
     |
     v
Select Approved Tools
     |
     v
Execute Reasoning
     |
     v
Validate Structured Output
     |
     v
Check Policy Requirements
     |
     v
Return Result
     |
     v
Record Execution Trace
```

### 7.4 Agent communication

Agents should communicate through structured messages, not arbitrary uncontrolled text exchanges.

Example:

```
{
  "message_id": "msg-001",
  "sender": "detection-agent",
  "recipient": "investigation-agent",
  "task_id": "task-102",
  "message_type": "security_finding",
  "priority": "high",
  "payload": {
    "event_id": "evt-101",
    "finding": "Suspicious authentication pattern",
    "confidence": 0.91
  },
  "timestamp": "2026-11-08T12:00:00Z"
}
```

This is an illustrative message, not a real security finding.

### 7.5 Agent execution trace

Every agent task should record:

- Input context.
- Context source.
- Model and version.
- Tool calls.
- Tool results.
- Execution timestamps.
- Output.
- Validation result.
- Approval requirements.
- Errors.
- Final status.

Never store secrets or unnecessary sensitive content in model traces.

## 8. Agent orchestration engine

This is one of the most important components.

The orchestrator decides:

- Which agent should handle a task.
- Which agents should work sequentially.
- Which agents can work in parallel.
- What information should be shared.
- What permissions are required.
- When human approval is necessary.
- What happens when an agent fails.

### 8.1 Example orchestration

Suppose a suspicious authentication event appears.

The orchestrator can create:

| Task | Agent                     | Dependency    |
| ---- | ------------------------- | ------------- |
| T1   | Detection Agent           | Initial event |
| T2   | Threat Intelligence Agent | T1            |
| T3   | Correlation Agent         | T1            |
| T4   | Investigation Agent       | T2 and T3     |
| T5   | Risk Assessment Agent     | T4            |
| T6   | Response Planner          | T5            |
| T7   | Human Approval            | T6            |
| T8   | Response Worker           | T7 approved   |

This is a dependency graph, not merely a list of chatbot calls.

### 8.2 Orchestrator responsibilities

- Task creation.
- Task assignment.
- Workflow state.
- Agent availability.
- Execution limits.
- Timeout handling.
- Retry policies.
- Failure recovery.
- Human-in-the-loop control.
- Cancellation.
- Event publishing.

Use a durable workflow approach for critical long-running tasks rather than relying on in-memory background tasks alone.

