## 22. End-to-end operational data flow

This is the most important technical workflow in HF-CDF.

[From Telemetry to Decisions: Building an AI-Driven Incident Intelligence System for Large-Scale Security Operations | by Mahesh Kuhile | Jun, 2026 | Medium](https://images.openai.com/static-rsc-4/8FYwoBRVLAwLU2dNPkDjCJuYO6_c14iK-2uyerPM1iEtFKt8vQ1BgSgNwOphUvTdA4DKI-tC-pmL4PqUBmTsJu9doND3VlZDknLPLBavgI-8WRkBPhyFDhol-YEVoLLWCwcvCnwU1Re9Uy2U-TOpdaG10DVMKTXp5Yuh2xhmwfo?purpose=inline)

[medium.com](https://medium.com/%40mahesh.kuhile/from-telemetry-to-decisions-building-an-ai-driven-incident-intelligence-system-for-large-scale-c9c62e427ddd?utm_source=chatgpt.com)

Consider a hypothetical suspicious authentication event.

### Step 1: Data collection

An authorized identity provider sends an event to HF-CDF.

### Step 2: Ingestion

The Event Service receives the event.

### Step 3: Normalization

The event is converted into the internal schema.

### Step 4: Detection

The Detection Service evaluates known detection rules.

### Step 5: Intelligence enrichment

Relevant context is retrieved from approved threat intelligence sources.

### Step 6: Correlation

The Correlation Agent searches for related events.

### Step 7: Investigation

The Investigation Agent examines the collected evidence.

### Step 8: Risk assessment

The Risk Analyst produces a structured assessment.

### Step 9: Response planning

The Response Planner generates possible defensive actions.

### Step 10: Approval

The platform checks whether human authorization is required.

### Step 11: Execution

The approved integration performs the authorized action.

### Step 12: Verification

The platform checks the action's actual outcome.

### Step 13: Reporting

The system creates an incident report and updates operational metrics.

### Step 14: Audit

All relevant actions, decisions and approvals are recorded.

This is the complete operational cycle the application should implement.

## 23. Frontend-to-backend interaction

Every important UI action should have an explicit backend flow.

Example: an operator opens an incident.

```
User opens incident page
          |
          v
Frontend route loads
          |
          v
Authentication checked
          |
          v
Incident API requested
          |
          v
Backend checks permission
          |
          v
PostgreSQL query
          |
          v
Incident + related evidence returned
          |
          v
Frontend renders incident
          |
          v
WebSocket subscribes to updates
          |
          v
Live authorized updates appear
```

For example, when an operator approves a response:

1. Frontend sends an approval request.
2. Backend verifies the user's permission.
3. Backend verifies that the request is still pending.
4. Policy engine checks the operation.
5. Approval record is persisted.
6. Orchestrator receives the approval event.
7. Execution worker processes the approved operation.
8. Execution result is recorded.
9. UI receives the updated state.

# PART XI — SECURITY, GOVERNANCE AND RELIABILITY

## 24. Security architecture

[Microsoft Zero Trust Model Explained: Key Principles](https://images.openai.com/static-rsc-4/96xBsNtjzPpqr6P0SRpviNYCT4PyCNPcgEEn35AKy8KOlmty6xfXJ1mWGX5Bmjo413HRJkkPI1UBpOuViCclqJ4_R6q4E3CLKIxP9D6z4yLgmd6BQ15_pM4dTU5anV2J3hUdFBSCSh1p9R0MajBBBLQtBDKmoedVvVDWhsl-OhA?purpose=inline)

[proserveit.com](https://www.proserveit.com/blog/what-is-microsoft-zero-trust-security-model?utm_source=chatgpt.com)

The platform itself is a security-sensitive system.

Its security cannot depend solely on AI agent instructions.

Enforce security in backend code, infrastructure, credentials, network boundaries and execution policies.

### 24.1 Authentication

Implement:

- Secure password hashing.
- MFA support.
- Session expiration.
- Secure cookies.
- Session revocation.
- Account recovery.
- Optional enterprise SSO.

### 24.2 Authorization

Implement role-based access control.

Example:

| Action                    | Analyst | Operator   | Admin | Auditor |
| ------------------------- | ------- | ---------- | ----- | ------- |
| View incidents            | Yes     | Yes        | Yes   | Yes     |
| Investigate               | Yes     | Yes        | Yes   | No      |
| Manage agents             | No      | Yes        | Yes   | No      |
| Approve sensitive actions | No      | Authorized | Yes   | No      |
| Manage users              | No      | No         | Yes   | No      |
| View audit records        | Limited | Limited    | Yes   | Yes     |
| Change security policies  | No      | No         | Yes   | No      |

Add environment-level and resource-level restrictions where required.

### 24.3 Agent security

Every agent needs:

- Explicit tool permissions.
- Task-scoped credentials.
- Input validation.
- Output validation.
- Execution timeouts.
- Resource limits.
- Tool-call auditing.
- Model-provider isolation.
- Untrusted-input handling.

The agent must never be able to override the platform's authorization policy through its system prompt.

### 24.4 AI-specific security

Account for:

- Prompt injection.
- Tool misuse.
- Excessive agency.
- Data leakage.
- Model output manipulation.
- Untrusted retrieval content.
- Cross-agent instruction contamination.
- Unauthorized action execution.

Use OWASP's 2026 Top 10 for Agentic Applications as one reference for the threat model.&#x20;

[image](https://www.google.com/s2/favicons?domain=https://genai.owasp.org\&sz=32)

OWASP Gen AI Security Project+1



### 24.5 Governance

Use NIST Cybersecurity Framework 2.0 as a governance reference.

Its six functions are:

- Govern.
- Identify.
- Protect.
- Detect.
- Respond.
- Recover.

These provide a useful structure for measuring the platform's security coverage.&#x20;

